---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'How do I contribute to Traverse?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'Start from labeled help-wanted and good-first-issue tickets across traverse-framework, follow CONTRIBUTING.md in the core traverse repo, and open a focused PR. Current starter work includes docs/MCP client configs, registry examples, and small website polish — not inventing new placement targets from scratch.',
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/how-do-i-install-traverse.html', label: 'How do I install Traverse?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-long-to-get-started.html', label: 'How long does it take to get started?' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/mcp-setup.html', label: 'MCP Server Setup guide' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md', label: 'CONTRIBUTING.md (core repo)' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I contribute to Traverse?"
  description="Point your first PR at labeled help-wanted issues, follow CONTRIBUTING.md, and skip the vague 'build a placement adapter' path until an issue asks for it."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-contribute-to-traverse.html"
  category="Project and Licensing"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>Traverse is open source (Apache-2.0). The useful first step is not inventing a new placement target from a blank page — it is picking a labeled ticket and shipping a small, reviewable change.</p>

  <h2>Start from labeled issues</h2>
  <p>Browse open tickets marked <code>help wanted</code> or <code>good first issue</code> across the org:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/search?q=org%3Atraverse-framework+is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3A%22help+wanted%22&amp;type=issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">org search: <code>help wanted</code></a></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/search?q=org%3Atraverse-framework+is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3A%22good+first+issue%22&amp;type=issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">org search: <code>good first issue</code></a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>A concrete starter batch (docs, examples, registry, site polish) lives here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1361" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse#1361</a> — docs / examples path</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1362" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse#1362</a> — docs / examples path</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/485" target="_blank" rel="noopener">registry#485</a> — registry capability / catalog work</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/website/issues/83" target="_blank" rel="noopener">website#83</a> — Contribute FAQ + MCP site snippets (this page)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Comment on the issue before starting so two people do not duplicate the same PR. If an issue is already claimed or closed, pick another from the searches above.</p>

  <h2>Read the first-PR path</h2>
  <p>Core repo expectations — CLA, branch hygiene, how to open a PR — are in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse/CONTRIBUTING.md</a>. Website and registry repos follow the same org governance; each has its own test command (<code>cargo test</code> / <code>npm test</code>).</p>

  <h2>What makes a good first PR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Docs and copy-paste configs that match the real CLI (especially MCP <code>stdio</code> launchers)</li>
    <li>Small examples that run as written</li>
    <li>One registry capability or catalog fix with clear validation notes</li>
    <li>Bug reports with a minimal reproduction</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to ask about before building</h2>
  <p>Large surface-area work — new placement executors, protocol changes, new crates — needs an issue or discussion first. Do not treat "placement target adapters" as the default first contribution; those are multi-PR platform projects. Open an issue, describe the gap, and wait for maintainer direction.</p>

  <h2>Pull request checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fork, branch, keep the change focused</li>
    <li>Run the repo's local tests before opening the PR</li>
    <li>Fill the PR template (<code>## Governing Spec</code>, <code>## Validation</code>, and project linkage when the template asks for them)</li>
    <li>Cite the issue you are closing</li>
  </ul>
</QuestionLayout>
